1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,290
In module six, you moved deals
through your pipeline by hand.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:07,770
That works, until things get
busy and cards stop moving.

3
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:12,057
In this lesson, workflows will keep
your pipeline up to date for you,

4
00:00:12,057 --> 00:00:15,723
and create tasks so your team
always knows what to do next.

5
00:00:16,571 --> 00:00:17,496
Here's the plan.

6
00:00:17,496 --> 00:00:20,155
When someone books a new
patient consultation,

7
00:00:20,155 --> 00:00:23,392
their deal should move to the
Consultation Booked stage,

8
00:00:23,392 --> 00:00:27,900
the contact should be tagged, and someone
should get a task to prepare for the

9
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:28,247
visit.

10
00:00:29,098 --> 00:00:33,810
Create a new workflow from scratch,
and name it so the purpose is obvious:

11
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:35,720
Consultation Booked, Pipeline.

12
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:39,275
The trigger is Customer
Booked Appointment.

13
00:00:39,275 --> 00:00:42,735
Add a filter for the New
Patient Consultation calendar,

14
00:00:42,735 --> 00:00:46,509
so check-ups and whitening
sessions don't move deals around.

15
00:00:46,509 --> 00:00:47,893
Then save the trigger.

16
00:00:49,088 --> 00:00:51,583
Now add Create, or Update Opportunity.

17
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:56,245
Choose the Patient Journey pipeline,
and the Consultation Booked stage.

18
00:00:56,245 --> 00:01:00,710
If the contact already has a deal
in this pipeline, Kyntlo moves it.

19
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:02,220
If not, it creates one.

20
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:04,781
So it works for brand-new bookings too.

21
00:01:05,631 --> 00:01:07,760
Two switches at the bottom matter.

22
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,265
Allow duplicate opportunities stays off,

23
00:01:10,265 --> 00:01:14,210
so one person never ends up with
three copies of the same deal.

24
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:18,155
And the first switch lets a deal
move back to an earlier stage,

25
00:01:18,155 --> 00:01:21,724
which is useful if someone
books again after going quiet.

26
00:01:21,724 --> 00:01:22,726
Save the action.

27
00:01:23,581 --> 00:01:26,508
Next, add a contact tag:
consultation booked.

28
00:01:26,508 --> 00:01:29,824
Tags like this are what
your other workflows check.

29
00:01:29,824 --> 00:01:32,816
Remember the If/Else in
our new lead workflow?

30
00:01:32,816 --> 00:01:34,767
It looks for exactly this tag.

31
00:01:35,624 --> 00:01:36,645
Finally, a task.

32
00:01:36,645 --> 00:01:41,752
Add Task, give it a clear title, like
Prepare consultation notes, and assign it.

33
00:01:41,752 --> 00:01:46,284
It appears in the assigned user's
task list, and on the contact record,

34
00:01:46,284 --> 00:01:47,369
due the next day.

35
00:01:48,221 --> 00:01:53,709
Here's the finished workflow: booking in,
deal moved, contact tagged, task created.

36
00:01:53,709 --> 00:01:55,692
Switch it to Publish and save.

37
00:01:55,692 --> 00:02:01,047
From now on, nobody has to remember to
drag that card, even on your busiest days.

38
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:05,110
Workflows can also start
from the pipeline itself.

39
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:08,256
Open the trigger list and
search for opportunity.

40
00:02:08,256 --> 00:02:13,201
You can react when a deal is created,
when its status changes to won or lost,

41
00:02:13,201 --> 00:02:16,283
when it moves to a stage,
or when it goes stale.

42
00:02:17,134 --> 00:02:19,075
Here's one Bloom already uses.

43
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:23,538
When an opportunity in the Patient
Journey pipeline is marked as won,

44
00:02:23,538 --> 00:02:27,742
the contact is tagged as a patient,
and receives a welcome email.

45
00:02:27,742 --> 00:02:31,494
A great first impression,
without anyone lifting a finger.

46
00:02:32,344 --> 00:02:33,401
Here's a pro tip.

47
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:38,004
Let workflows handle the predictable
moves in the middle of your pipeline,

48
00:02:38,004 --> 00:02:39,435
like enquiry to booked.

49
00:02:39,435 --> 00:02:42,110
But let a person mark
deals as won or lost.

50
00:02:42,110 --> 00:02:46,402
That keeps your reports honest, and
your revenue numbers trustworthy.

51
00:02:47,261 --> 00:02:49,337
Your pipeline now updates itself.

52
00:02:49,337 --> 00:02:53,803
In the next lesson, you'll learn how
to start new workflows in seconds,

53
00:02:53,803 --> 00:02:55,376
using ready-made recipes.

54
00:02:55,376 --> 00:02:56,256
See you there!
